NAME
    abi - the ROM vectors, the OS addresses and the syscall numbers
          (#include "../lib_abi.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_abi.c"       from a command in os/commands/
    (lib_fs.c includes it: most commands include lib_fs.c instead)

DESCRIPTION
    #defines that name every address a program or the OS relies on, so
    a command writes bios(CHAROUT, 0, c) or sys(SYS_OPEN, path), never
    a raw number. A #define costs no code.

    ROM vectors (firmware/abi/README.md), for y1cc's bios(addr, r7,
    acc): R7 and ACC are set, the routine is called, ACC comes back.
        STRINGOUT $FFC0  print the string at R7
        CHAROUT   $FFC4  print the byte in ACC  (UARTOUT $FFC8: same)
        SHOWADDR  $FFCC  print R7 as four hex digits and ": "
        UARTIN    $FFE8  a console byte (waits; echoes on the machine)
        CFINIT    $FFEC  initialise the CF card; ACC 0 = ok
        CFREAD    $FFF0  sector CFLBA0..2 into the 512 bytes at R7
        CFWRITE   $FFF4  sector CFLBA0..2 from R7; ACC 0 = ok
        CONST     $FFF8  1 when a console byte is waiting
        UARTINNE  $FFFC  a console byte without echo

    Video (ROM 2026-09-25 and later): VIDCTL $FFBC, the entry below
    the full vector table: bios(VIDCTL, 0, n), n = 0 probe, 1 init,
    2 clear; ACC = VIDPRES. Check peek(VIDCTL) == VIDSIG ($04) first
    (an older ROM has $FF there). VIDPRES $0FF0 (1 = card found at
    reset), VIDMIR $0FF1 (nonzero = CHAROUT also writes the screen),
    VIDCUR $0FF2, VROW $0FF3, VCOL $0FF4 (see video).

    RAM: SYSARG0..2 $0F06/$0F08/$0F0A and SYSRES $0F0C (the syscall
    block), CFLBA0..2 $0F10..$0F12, SYSTAB $0F14 (entries 0..21),
    SYSTAB2 $4FC0 (entries 0..31, 2026-09-25; SYS_OLD = 21),
    ARGBUF $0F40 (ARGMAX = 127 characters + NUL: argstr()), OSBASE
    $1000, TPA $5000 and TPATOP $D000 (the program area).

    Syscalls: sys(n, a, b, c) puts a, b, c in SYSARG0..2, calls the
    word at SYSTAB + 2n (n 0..21; 22..31 at SYSTAB2 + 2n) and returns
    SYSRES, a full 16-bit word:
         0 SYS_OPEN      8 SYS_MKDIR    16 SYS_ENTRY
         1 SYS_READ      9 SYS_RMDIR    17 SYS_CONIN
         2 SYS_GETC     10 SYS_OPENDIR  18 SYS_CONST
         3 SYS_CLOSE    11 SYS_READDIR  19 SYS_CONOUT
                                        20 SYS_KEYIN
                                        21 SYS_STDIO  (SYSTAB's last)
         4 SYS_CREATE   12 SYS_RESOLVE
         5 SYS_WRITE    13 SYS_GETCWD
         6 SYS_PUTC     14 SYS_CHDIR
         7 SYS_DELETE   15 SYS_RENAME
    and in SYSTAB2 only (2026-09-25):
        22 SYS_EXIT     23 SYS_EXEC     24 SYS_SEEK
        25 SYS_READN (2026-09-25)
    STATUS $0F0E: the status the last program left (0, or EXIT's).
    CONIN/CONST are stdin and CONOUT stdout, which the shell redirects
    (y1cc --os compiles getchar/putchar/puts to them); KEYIN is always
    the keyboard; STDIO says which are redirected (2026-09-23). A
    raw bios(CHAROUT, ...) bypasses redirection: errors only (err).
    Their arguments and results are in os/README.md (md OS.MD); the C
    wrappers are in lib_fs.c (see fs). Y1/OS fills SYSTAB at boot; the
    handlers read SYSARGn with peekw() and write SYSRES with pokew().

NOTES
    There is no carry bit: every result is a word, 65535 meaning none.
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23 (the ROM names date from 2026-09-22). It
    replaces P8X os/commands/lib_abi.c, whose BIOS jump table, OS
    syscall vector and & 256 carry tests do not exist here.

